msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:395
msgid "The input format comes from ``--from`` or the input's suffix: ``.rst`` and ``.rest`` are reStructuredText, ``.md`` and ``.markdown`` CommonMark, ``.typ`` Typst. A Markdown file is plain CommonMark 0.31.2. ``--from myst`` reads it as MyST with the Sphinx layer instead, so roles such as ``{doc}`` and ``{ref}`` and directives such as ``{note}`` are understood. ``--from sphinx`` likewise reads reStructuredText with Sphinx's directives, roles, and domains. The output format comes from ``--to`` or the output's suffix (``.html``, ``.htm``, ``.pdf``, ``.rst``, ``.rest``). Only the options below apply to ``convert``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:403
msgid "``--to rst`` writes reStructuredText with Guidedog's own writer (the ``rst`` renderer). It keeps the author's lines: each line break inside a source paragraph stays a line break, and the writer adds none of its own, so a converted page keeps the sentence and paragraph shape we wrote. It never splits inline markup, inline code, a role or a URL across lines. It uses one section adornment per level (``=``, ``-``, ``~``, ``^``, ...). It keeps roles, cross-references, and directives as Sphinx writes them. It reads the input without smart quotes, so quotes and dashes stay as written. Some things reStructuredText cannot express exactly (a hard line break, an image inside text, a link title). The writer comes as close as it can and reports a note. Typst input cannot be converted to reStructuredText."
msgstr ""

