msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:629
msgid "guidedog plugin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:632
msgid "Records which plugin programs may run on this machine, in ``plugins.toml`` in Guidedog's configuration folder (``GUIDEDOG_CONFIG_DIR`` moves it):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:645
msgid "Subcommands:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:647
msgid "``register ID PATH [--project=DIR] [--config=FILE] [--timeout=SECONDS] [--yes]``: inspects the program after we confirm (``--yes`` skips the question) and records its SHA-256. The program must say it is ``ID``. A build plugin passes the qualification checks below before it is registered."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:651
msgid "``check PATH [--project=DIR] [--config=FILE] [--timeout=SECONDS]``: runs the qualification checks against a build plugin without changing the registry. A publisher is refused. We test a publisher with its own tests and ``guidedog publish --dry-run``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:654
msgid "``list``: the registered programs, their IDs, paths, and kinds, beside the plugins linked into this executable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:656
msgid "``inspect ID``: an entry's declared build hooks (source, HTML, assets) or publisher capabilities, the commands it adds, and whether the program on disk is still the one registered."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:659
msgid "``run ID -- [ARGS...]``: runs a registered program's own commands, after checking its SHA-256."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:661
msgid "``unregister ID``: removes the entry from ``plugins.toml``. The program, and anything it published, stay unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:664
msgid "Build plugins are selected by ``plugins`` in ``conf.toml`` and run during ``guidedog build``. ``guidedog publish`` accepts publishers only. ``--untrusted`` disables build plugins. See :doc:`../plugins` for the complete guide, an example, and configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:668
msgid "Build-plugin qualification checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:671
msgid "``guidedog plugin check PATH --project=DIR --config=FILE`` exercises the declared hooks in child processes: loading, repeated results, and cleanup. ``--project`` defaults to the current folder. A relative ``--config`` is relative to it. The request timeout defaults to 60 seconds. The program's SHA-256 must be the same before and after the checks. Publisher registration does not invoke publication. Passing checks is interoperability evidence, not a sandbox or a safety certificate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:678
msgid "Custom binaries with linked plugins"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:681
msgid "The whole command line is the ``gd-cli`` crate. A Rust program can be a ``guidedog`` with plugins linked in, instead of registered as separate programs. It passes a ``gd_plugins::Registry`` of its plugins to ``gd_cli::run_with``. That function reads the process arguments and returns the exit status."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:700
msgid "The program accepts every command and option on this page. A selected plugin ID is looked up among the linked plugins first, then in the machine's registry. Linked plugins need no registration and no SHA-256 pin, because they are part of the executable. ``gd_cli::run`` is the same with no linked plugins. ``guidedog`` itself links one, the GitHub Pages plugin. The plugin is the same ``Plugin`` value its installed form serves with ``gd_plugins::serve_plugin`` (see :doc:`../plugins`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:707
msgid "Commands plugins add"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:710
msgid "A plugin may add commands to guidedog, or targets to ``guidedog publish``. ``guidedog --help`` lists them under PLUGIN COMMANDS, each with the ID of its plugin, and ``guidedog plugin inspect ID`` shows a plugin's:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:720
msgid "guidedog looks for a word it does not know (or a ``publish`` target) among the commands of the linked plugins first, then among those of the registered programs, and runs the plugin that adds it. ``--help`` after such a command prints its help, made from what the plugin declares. A plugin cannot replace one of guidedog's commands, and the first plugin to add a name keeps it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:722
msgid "A linked plugin's command runs inside guidedog. A registered program's command runs as that program, started on our terminal after its SHA-256 is checked: it may ask questions, and its exit status is the command's. When either asks for a build, guidedog builds the project. The program's commands are read when it is registered, so a new version is registered again, as always."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:724
msgid "guidedog-pages"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/cli.rst:727
msgid "Publishes Guidedog sites to GitHub Pages from this machine. It builds projects with the installed ``guidedog``. ``publish`` does any missing setup step first, as ``guidedog publish pages`` does. The step commands stay for scripts and for checking each part. See :doc:`../howto/github-pages`."
